CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 6
Women and children victims of acts of violence shall receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. the right to recovery of health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and
j. the right to assistance.

CHAPTER VIII
SPECIAL PROTECTION OF CHILDREN
Article 15
(1) Special protection of children is provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children victims of sexual crimes;
k. children victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(2) The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 17
(1) The forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal assistance;
d. rehabilitation and social reintegration;
e. repatriation;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
(3) The forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by related OPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(4) In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with government agencies, other regional governments, non-governmental organizations, and social institutions.
(5) Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling services for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 14
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 13, is implemented…. implemented by OPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
